>
  <div class="quick-answer">
    <div class="quick-answer-label">Quick Answer</div>
    <p>For parents with minor children, an estate plan does two things nothing else can: it names who raises your kids if you can't, and it controls how your assets support them. A complete family plan typically includes a <a href="/services/wills-and-trusts">revocable living trust</a>, pour-over will with guardianship designations, <a href="/services/financial-power-of-attorney">financial power of attorney</a>, and <a href="/services/healthcare-directive">healthcare directive</a> — plus life insurance coordination and beneficiary designation reviews.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <p>Having children fundamentally changes what's at stake in estate planning. Before kids, planning is primarily about assets and decision-making authority. After kids, it's about people — small people who depend entirely on you, and who need both a caretaker and financial protection if something happens.</p>

    <p>Arizona courts use a "best interests of the child" standard when appointing guardians for children whose parents have died or become incapacitated. Without a guardianship designation, the court makes that decision based on who petitions and what evidence is available. Family members may disagree. The process is public, potentially contested, and stressful for everyone involved — especially the children.</p>

    <p>A guardianship designation in a properly executed will is the only legal mechanism that gives you a say. It takes less than an hour to prepare and costs a fraction of what a contested guardianship proceeding costs your family.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>What Growing Families Need</h2>
    <p><strong>Guardianship Designations.</strong> Names who raises your minor children — and in what order if your first choice can't serve. This is drafted in your will and is the single most important document for any parent.</p>

    <p><strong>Revocable Living Trust with Minor's Provisions.</strong> Controls how assets are managed and distributed for your children's benefit. Most parents don't want a 19-year-old inheriting a lump sum. The trust can hold assets and distribute for education, health, and support until the children reach an age you choose — 25, 30, 35, or in stages.</p>

    <p><strong>Pour-Over Will.</strong> Catches any assets not titled in the trust and directs them into it. Also the document where guardianship designations live under Arizona law.</p>

    <p><strong>Life Insurance Review.</strong> Life insurance is often the largest asset for young families. Making sure the policy is owned correctly, the beneficiary is your trust (not individual children), and the coverage amount reflects your family's actual needs is a critical part of the plan.</p>

    <p><strong>Financial Power of Attorney.</strong> If you're incapacitated but not deceased, someone needs authority to pay the mortgage, manage investments, and handle your children's financial needs. A <a href="/services/financial-power-of-attorney">financial power of attorney</a> provides that authority without a court proceeding.</p>

    <p><strong>Healthcare Directive.</strong> Names your spouse (and a backup) as your healthcare agent. For couples with children, this is especially important — if both parents are in the same accident, clear documentation prevents delays in medical decision-making.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>Trust Distributions for Children</h2>
    <p>One of the most important decisions in a family estate plan is when and how children receive their inheritance. Arizona law allows minors to receive up to $10,000 under the Uniform Transfers to Minors Act without a trust, but anything beyond that amount — or any asset requiring management — should be held in trust.</p>

    <p>We typically recommend a common trust that holds all assets for all children until the youngest reaches a specified age, then splits into individual shares. Distribution stages — a third at 25, a third at 30, the remainder at 35 — are popular because they give young adults access to funds as they mature while protecting against impulsive decisions. But every family is different, and we design the structure around your values and your children's needs.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>What It Costs</h2>
    <p>Family estate plans are flat-fee, quoted after your <a href="/contact">free consultation</a>. You'll know the total cost before committing. Most families complete the process in two to three appointments.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-section">
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
          What if my spouse and I disagree on who should be guardian?
          <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>It happens more often than you'd think, and it's better to work through it now than to leave it unresolved. We facilitate that conversation during the planning process. If you and your spouse name different people in separate documents, a court weighs both — which creates exactly the kind of uncertainty a coordinated plan is designed to prevent.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
          Should my life insurance beneficiary be my spouse or my trust?
          <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>In most cases, naming your trust as beneficiary is the better approach. It ensures the life insurance proceeds are managed according to the trust's distribution plan — which includes provisions for your children, age-based distributions, and trustee oversight. Naming your spouse directly means the proceeds pass to them outright, which may not be the best structure if both spouses die simultaneously or if you have <a href="/life-stages/blended-families">blended family</a> considerations.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
          At what age should I set up my child's inheritance distribution?
          <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>There's no single right answer, but most parents we work with choose distributions in stages — commonly a third at 25, a third at 30, and the remainder at 35. This gives young adults access to funds as they mature while protecting against inexperience. Some parents choose a single distribution age, and others tie distributions to milestones like completing a degree. We design around what matters to your family.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
          We already have a plan from before we had kids. Is it still valid?
          <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>It may be legally valid, but it almost certainly doesn't reflect your current needs. A plan created before you had children won't include guardianship designations, minor's trust provisions, or life insurance coordination. We review existing plans regularly — the consultation is the right starting point.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <script>
    function toggleFaq(btn) {
      const item = btn.closest('.faq-item');
      const isOpen = item.classList.contains('open');
      document.querySelectorAll('.faq-item.open').forEach(el => el.classList.remove('open'));
      if (!isOpen) item.classList.add('open');
    }
  </script>
